</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.2 </span>Defense against Indirect Prompt Injection Attacks</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the defense against indirect prompt injection attacks, which are more practical, using both the Inj-SQuAD and Inj-TriviaQA datasets. The results are presented in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.3.2 Defense against Indirect Prompt Injection Attacks ‣ 4.3 Main Results and Analysis ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Robustness via Referencing: Defending against Prompt Injection Attacks by Referencing the Executed Instruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.3.2 Defense against Indirect Prompt Injection Attacks ‣ 4.3 Main Results and Analysis ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Robustness via Referencing: Defending against Prompt Injection Attacks by Referencing the Executed Instruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Our findings show that our method remains effective against indirect prompt injection attacks, achieving a maximum ASR of only 4.00% on the Inj-SQuAD dataset and 7.00% on the Inj-TriviaQA dataset. In contrast, prompt-engineering-based methods are significantly less effective, with the lowest ASR reaching 19.67% for the Inj-SQuAD dataset. Compared to direct prompt injection attacks, the defense performance of StruQ against known attacks is better. However, it still fails to successfully defend against unknown attacks such as “Fakecom.” In contrast, our method consistently defends against all baseline attacks.</p>
</div>
